Object.assign((window.COI_EXPLAIN = window.COI_EXPLAIN || {}), {
  "152": { "s": "Defines \"State\" for Part VI, which deals with State governments.", "e": "This is a definition clause for Part VI (Articles 152–237), which sets up the government of each State. The official text still says that \"State\" here does not include Jammu and Kashmir, a relic of the time when J&K had its own constitution under Article 370. Since 2019 Jammu and Kashmir has been reorganised into Union territories, so the exclusion no longer has practical effect." },
  "153": { "s": "Every State must have a Governor; one person may serve several States.", "e": "Each State has a Governor, who is the formal head of the State, much as the President is for the Union. A proviso added by the Seventh Amendment (1956) allows the same person to be Governor of two or more States at once, which is common in practice when a post falls vacant." },
  "154": { "s": "The State's executive power is vested in the Governor.", "e": "All executive power of a State is formally held by the Governor, who exercises it directly or through subordinate officers. In practice, because of Article 163, the Governor acts on the advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister; the Supreme Court in Shamsher Singh v. State of Punjab (1974) held that the Governor is a constitutional head and real power lies with the elected ministry. The article does not automatically transfer to the Governor powers that existing laws give to other authorities, and legislatures can still give functions to officers below the Governor." },
  "155": { "s": "The President appoints the Governor of each State.", "e": "Governors are not elected; they are appointed by the President through a formal warrant. Since the President acts on the advice of the Union Council of Ministers, the choice of Governor is effectively made by the Central Government." },
  "156": { "s": "Governor holds office at the President's pleasure, normally for five years.", "e": "The Governor's term is nominally five years, and a Governor continues until a successor takes over. But the Governor holds office 'during the pleasure of the President', so the Union can remove a Governor at any time. In B.P. Singhal v. Union of India (2010), the Supreme Court held that this power cannot be used arbitrarily, for example merely because a Governor is out of step with the policies of a new Union government, and that removal can be challenged if it is shown to be mala fide or arbitrary. A Governor may also resign by writing to the President." },
  "157": { "s": "A Governor must be an Indian citizen aged at least 35.", "e": "Only two qualifications are laid down: Indian citizenship and a minimum age of thirty-five years. There is no educational or other requirement in the Constitution." },
  "158": { "s": "Governor cannot hold a legislative seat or other paid office; gets residence and emoluments.", "e": "A Governor cannot be a member of Parliament or of any State Legislature; if a sitting legislator is appointed, the seat is treated as vacated on taking office. The Governor cannot hold any other office of profit. The Governor gets a rent-free official residence and emoluments fixed by Parliament, which cannot be reduced during the term. Where one person is Governor of two or more States, the cost is shared among them as the President decides (a rule added by the Seventh Amendment, 1956)." },
  "159": { "s": "Governor takes an oath to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution.", "e": "Before taking office, a Governor (or anyone acting as Governor) takes an oath before the Chief Justice of the State's High Court, or the senior-most available judge. The Governor swears to faithfully perform the office, to preserve, protect and defend the Constitution and the law, and to serve the welfare of the people of the State." },
  "160": { "s": "President may provide for Governor's functions in unforeseen situations.", "e": "If a situation arises that the Constitution does not cover, for example the sudden death of a Governor, the President can make arrangements for someone to carry out the Governor's duties. In practice this is how a Chief Justice of the High Court or the Governor of a neighbouring State is often asked to take temporary charge." },
  "161": { "s": "Governor can pardon or reduce sentences for offences under State-subject laws.", "e": "The Governor can grant pardons, reprieves, respites or remissions, or suspend, remit or commute sentences, for offences against laws on matters within the State's executive power. The President's parallel power under Article 72 is wider, since it also covers court-martial sentences and every death sentence. Courts have held that this power is exercised on the advice of the State Council of Ministers and is open to limited judicial review, for example if a decision is arbitrary or based on irrelevant considerations (Epuru Sudhakar v. Government of A.P., 2006)." },
  "162": { "s": "State executive power covers matters on which the State Legislature can make laws.", "e": "A State Government's executive power extends to everything on which the State Legislature can legislate, mainly the State List and the Concurrent List. For Concurrent List matters, however, the State's executive power is subject to and limited by executive powers that the Constitution or a Parliamentary law expressly gives to the Union. This keeps State executive action in step with the federal division of legislative powers." },
  "163": { "s": "A Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister aids and advises the Governor.", "e": "This is the core of parliamentary government in the States. The Governor must act on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers headed by the Chief Minister, except where the Constitution requires the Governor to act in his discretion. Clause (2) says the Governor's own decision on whether a matter falls within his discretion is final, and clause (3) bars courts from inquiring into what advice ministers gave. However, the Supreme Court has read discretion narrowly: in Shamsher Singh (1974) it held the Governor is a constitutional head bound by ministerial advice, and in Nabam Rebia v. Deputy Speaker (2016) it held that discretion exists only where the Constitution expressly or by necessary implication provides for it, and that the Governor cannot, for example, advance an Assembly session on his own. Recognised areas of discretion include reporting to the President under Article 356, reserving certain Bills for the President, and special responsibilities under some Schedules and Article 371 provisions." },
  "164": { "s": "Chief Minister and Ministers: appointment, size limit, collective responsibility and oath.", "e": "The Governor appoints the Chief Minister, and other Ministers on the Chief Minister's advice; ministers hold office at the Governor's pleasure, which in practice means as long as they have the confidence of the Assembly. Chhattisgarh, Jharkhand, Madhya Pradesh and Odisha must have a Minister for tribal welfare (Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand replaced Bihar by the 94th Amendment, 2006). The 91st Amendment (2003) capped the Council of Ministers at 15% of the Assembly's strength, with a minimum of twelve, and barred legislators disqualified for defection under the Tenth Schedule from becoming ministers until their term ends or they are re-elected. The Council is collectively responsible to the Legislative Assembly, and the Supreme Court in S.R. Bommai v. Union of India (1994) stressed that whether a ministry has majority support should be tested on the floor of the House. A non-member can be a Minister for up to six consecutive months but must get elected within that time. Salaries of ministers are fixed by the State Legislature." },
  "165": { "s": "The Governor appoints an Advocate-General as the State's chief law officer.", "e": "Each State has an Advocate-General, appointed by the Governor, who must be qualified to be a High Court judge. The Advocate-General advises the State Government on legal matters and performs legal duties assigned by the Governor. The post is held at the Governor's pleasure, so in practice the Advocate-General usually changes when the government changes." },
  "166": { "s": "State government acts are taken in the Governor's name under business rules.", "e": "All executive action of a State Government is formally expressed to be taken in the name of the Governor, and orders authenticated as the rules require cannot be challenged on the ground that the Governor did not personally make them. The Governor makes 'Rules of Business' to allocate work among ministers and departments, except for matters where the Governor acts in his discretion. In practice, most decisions are taken by ministers and officers under these rules." },
  "167": { "s": "Chief Minister must keep the Governor informed about decisions and proposed laws.", "e": "The Chief Minister must communicate to the Governor all Cabinet decisions on administration and proposals for legislation, and provide any information the Governor asks for on these. If the Governor requires, the Chief Minister must place before the full Council of Ministers a matter decided by a single Minister alone. This gives the Governor a right to be informed and to advise, without giving him power to override the ministry." },
  "168": { "s": "Each State Legislature has the Governor plus one or two Houses.", "e": "Every State Legislature consists of the Governor and either one House (the Legislative Assembly) or two Houses (the Assembly and a Legislative Council). The list of States with two Houses has changed over time through Acts of Parliament; the current text lists Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Telangana and Uttar Pradesh. Note that a Council exists only after Parliament actually creates it under Article 169, so the list in the text may not exactly match the Councils functioning at any given time." },
  "169": { "s": "Parliament can abolish or create a State's Legislative Council at the Assembly's request.", "e": "A State's Legislative Council can be created or abolished by an ordinary law of Parliament, but only if the State's Legislative Assembly first passes a resolution by a majority of its total membership and two-thirds of those present and voting. Such a law is not treated as a constitutional amendment under Article 368, even though it changes the list in Article 168. Several Councils have been abolished and revived this way, for example in Andhra Pradesh." },
  "170": { "s": "Legislative Assemblies have 60–500 directly elected members; seats are delimited by population.", "e": "Each State Assembly has between 60 and 500 members elected directly from territorial constituencies, with roughly equal population per seat across the State (some small States have fewer than 60 under special provisions elsewhere). Seats are to be readjusted after each census. However, through the 42nd, 84th and 87th Amendments, the total number of seats per State is frozen on the 1971 census, and constituency boundaries are based on the 2001 census, until figures of the first census after 2026 are published. This freeze was meant to avoid penalising States that controlled population growth." },
  "171": { "s": "Legislative Council size and how its members are chosen.", "e": "A Legislative Council can have at most one-third of the Assembly's strength (raised from one-fourth by the Seventh Amendment, 1956) and at least 40 members. Roughly one-third are elected by local bodies, one-twelfth by graduates, one-twelfth by teachers, one-third by the Assembly from non-members, and the rest are nominated by the Governor from people with expertise in literature, science, art, co-operation or social service. Elections are by proportional representation through the single transferable vote. Parliament can change this composition by law." },
  "172": { "s": "Assemblies last five years; Legislative Councils are permanent.", "e": "A Legislative Assembly lasts five years from its first meeting unless dissolved earlier. The 42nd Amendment (1976) extended this to six years, but the 44th Amendment (1978) restored five years. During a national Emergency, Parliament can extend an Assembly's life by up to one year at a time, but not beyond six months after the Emergency ends. A Legislative Council is never dissolved; one-third of its members retire every two years." },
  "173": { "s": "Qualifications to become a member of a State Legislature.", "e": "A candidate must be an Indian citizen, take an oath or affirmation before an Election Commission-authorised person, and be at least 25 years old for the Assembly or 30 for the Council. Parliament can add further qualifications by law, which it has done in the Representation of the People Acts. The oath requirement was added by the 16th Amendment (1963) to make candidates affirm allegiance to India's sovereignty and integrity." },
  "174": { "s": "Governor summons, prorogues and dissolves the State Legislature.", "e": "The Governor calls the Legislature into session, ends a session (prorogation) and can dissolve the Assembly. The gap between two sessions cannot exceed six months, so the Legislature must meet at least twice a year. These powers are ordinarily exercised on the advice of the Council of Ministers; the Supreme Court in Nabam Rebia (2016) held that a Governor cannot summon or advance a session on his own while the ministry enjoys majority support." },
  "175": { "s": "Governor may address the Legislature and send it messages.", "e": "The Governor may address the Assembly, or either or both Houses, and require members to attend. The Governor can also send messages, including about pending Bills, and the House must consider them promptly. In practice these messages are rare and generally reflect the government's position." },
  "176": { "s": "Governor's special address at the start of the first session each year.", "e": "At the first session after every general election and at the start of the first session each year, the Governor must address the Legislature and explain why it has been summoned. This address is written by the State Government and sets out its policies and programme. The House rules must provide time to discuss it, through a motion of thanks. The First Amendment (1951) limited this address to these occasions instead of every session." },
  "177": { "s": "Ministers and the Advocate-General can speak in either House but vote only where members.", "e": "Every Minister and the Advocate-General can speak and take part in proceedings of both Houses and of committees they are named to. But this article does not give them a vote; a Minister can vote only in the House of which he or she is a member." },
  "178": { "s": "Each Assembly elects a Speaker and a Deputy Speaker.", "e": "Each Legislative Assembly must choose two of its members as Speaker and Deputy Speaker as soon as possible, and fill any vacancy. The Speaker presides over the House and has important powers, including deciding defection cases under the Tenth Schedule." },
  "179": { "s": "How a Speaker or Deputy Speaker vacates office, resigns, or is removed.", "e": "The Speaker or Deputy Speaker loses office on ceasing to be an Assembly member, may resign in writing, or may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members of the Assembly after 14 days' notice. When the Assembly is dissolved, the Speaker stays in office until just before the new Assembly first meets, ensuring continuity. In Nabam Rebia (2016) the Supreme Court held that a Speaker facing a pending removal notice should not decide disqualification petitions under the Tenth Schedule; a larger bench was later asked to reconsider this view." },
  "180": { "s": "Who acts as Speaker when the office is vacant or the Speaker is absent.", "e": "If the Speaker's office is vacant, the Deputy Speaker performs its duties, and if both are vacant, a member appointed by the Governor does so. When the Speaker is merely absent from a sitting, the Deputy Speaker presides, or else someone determined under the House's rules (usually a panel of chairpersons) or by the Assembly itself." },
  "181": { "s": "Speaker or Deputy Speaker cannot preside while their own removal is debated.", "e": "When a resolution to remove the Speaker or Deputy Speaker is being considered, that person cannot preside, even if present. The Speaker can still speak and take part in the proceedings and can vote in the first instance on the resolution, but not in case of a tie (no casting vote)." },
  "182": { "s": "Each Legislative Council elects a Chairman and Deputy Chairman.", "e": "A State with a Legislative Council must have a Chairman and Deputy Chairman, chosen by the Council from among its members. Unlike the Rajya Sabha, where the Vice-President is ex officio Chairman, the State Council elects its own Chairman." },
  "183": { "s": "How a Council's Chairman or Deputy Chairman vacates office, resigns, or is removed.", "e": "The Chairman or Deputy Chairman of a Legislative Council loses office on ceasing to be a member, may resign in writing, or may be removed by a resolution passed by a majority of all the then members of the Council after at least 14 days' notice. This mirrors Article 179 for the Assembly." },
  "184": { "s": "Who acts as Council Chairman when the post is vacant or the Chairman is absent.", "e": "If the Chairman's office is vacant, the Deputy Chairman acts, or if both are vacant, a member appointed by the Governor. When the Chairman is merely absent from a sitting, the Deputy Chairman, a person named under the Council's rules, or someone chosen by the Council presides." },
  "185": { "s": "Council Chairman or Deputy cannot preside while their own removal is debated.", "e": "During consideration of a resolution to remove the Chairman or Deputy Chairman of a Legislative Council, that person may not preside. The Chairman can speak and vote in the first instance on the resolution, but has no casting vote on it. This mirrors Article 181." },
  "186": { "s": "Salaries of the Speaker, Deputy Speaker, Chairman and Deputy Chairman.", "e": "The salaries and allowances of the presiding officers of State Legislatures are fixed by a law of the State Legislature. Until such a law is made, the amounts in the Second Schedule apply." },
  "187": { "s": "Each House of a State Legislature has its own separate secretariat.", "e": "Each House has its own secretarial staff, so that the Legislature is independent of the executive government, though some posts can be common to both Houses. The State Legislature can regulate recruitment and service conditions by law; until then, the Governor may make rules after consulting the Speaker or Chairman." },
  "188": { "s": "Members must take an oath before taking their seats.", "e": "Every member of a State Legislative Assembly or Council must take an oath or affirmation in the form set out in the Third Schedule, before the Governor or a person the Governor appoints (usually the pro-tem Speaker), before taking their seat. Without it, a member cannot sit or vote (see Article 193)." },
  "189": { "s": "Decisions by majority vote; casting vote for presiding officer; quorum rules.", "e": "Questions in a House are decided by a majority of members present and voting, except where the Constitution requires a special majority. The presiding officer does not vote in the first instance but has a casting vote in a tie. A House can function despite vacancies, and proceedings are not invalid just because someone not entitled to sit took part. The quorum is ten members or one-tenth of the House, whichever is greater, and without a quorum the presiding officer must adjourn or suspend the sitting." },
  "190": { "s": "When a member's seat falls vacant: dual membership, resignation, absence, disqualification.", "e": "No one can be a member of both Houses of a State Legislature, or of the Legislatures of two States, at once; the person must give up one seat within the time set by rules. A seat becomes vacant if the member becomes disqualified or resigns, but under the 33rd Amendment (1974) the Speaker or Chairman accepts a resignation only if satisfied it is voluntary and genuine. A House may also declare a seat vacant if a member is absent without permission from all meetings for sixty days. Since the 52nd Amendment (1985) this article also covers disqualification for defection under the Tenth Schedule." },
  "191": { "s": "Grounds for disqualification from a State Legislature, including defection.", "e": "A person is disqualified from being a member of a State Legislature if he holds an office of profit under the Union or a State Government (unless the State Legislature has exempted that office by law), is declared of unsound mind by a court, is an undischarged insolvent, is not an Indian citizen or has acquired foreign citizenship or allegiance, or is disqualified under a law of Parliament (for example, on criminal conviction under the Representation of the People Act, 1951). Being a Minister is not an office of profit. Clause (2), added by the 52nd Amendment (1985), disqualifies members who defect under the Tenth Schedule; such cases are decided by the Speaker or Chairman, not the Governor." },
  "192": { "s": "Governor decides disqualification questions, but must follow the Election Commission's opinion.", "e": "If a question arises whether a member has become disqualified under Article 191(1) (office of profit, insolvency, citizenship, etc.), it goes to the Governor, whose decision is final. Before deciding, the Governor must get the Election Commission's opinion and act according to it, so the Commission's view is effectively binding. Defection cases under the Tenth Schedule are not covered here; they are decided by the presiding officer." },
  "193": { "s": "Penalty of Rs 500 a day for sitting or voting when not entitled.", "e": "A person who sits or votes in a State Legislature without first taking the oath, or knowing that he is not qualified, is disqualified, or is barred by law, is liable to a penalty of five hundred rupees for each day, recoverable as a debt owed to the State." },
  "194": { "s": "Freedom of speech and legislative privileges of State Legislatures and members.", "e": "There is freedom of speech in State Legislatures, subject to the Constitution and House rules. Members cannot be sued or prosecuted in any court for anything said or any vote given in the Legislature or its committees, and the same protection covers official publications of the House. Other privileges are those defined by law, and until then those the House had before the 44th Amendment came into force (historically modelled on the British House of Commons). Courts have held that these privileges must be read in harmony with fundamental rights, and in Sita Soren v. Union of India (2024) a seven-judge bench held that taking a bribe to vote or speak is not protected by legislative privilege." },
  "195": { "s": "Salaries and allowances of State legislators are fixed by State law.", "e": "Members of the State Legislative Assembly and Council get salaries and allowances determined by a law of the State Legislature. Until such a law was made, pre-Constitution rates for provincial legislators applied." },
  "196": { "s": "How Bills are introduced and passed; when pending Bills lapse.", "e": "In a State with two Houses, an ordinary Bill can start in either House, but Money Bills and certain financial Bills must start in the Assembly. A Bill is passed only when both Houses agree to it, subject to the Assembly's overriding power under Articles 197 and 198. Prorogation does not kill a pending Bill. Dissolution of the Assembly causes Bills pending in, or passed by, the Assembly to lapse, but a Bill pending in the Council that the Assembly has not passed survives." },
  "197": { "s": "Legislative Council can only delay ordinary Bills, not block them.", "e": "If the Council rejects an Assembly-passed Bill, sits on it for more than three months, or makes amendments the Assembly does not accept, the Assembly can pass it again. If the Council again rejects it, delays it for more than one month, or insists on unacceptable amendments, the Bill is deemed passed in the form the Assembly passed it the second time. Unlike Parliament, there is no joint sitting; the Council can at most delay a Bill by a few months." },
  "198": { "s": "Money Bills start in the Assembly; the Council can only recommend within 14 days.", "e": "A Money Bill cannot be introduced in the Legislative Council. After the Assembly passes it, the Council must return it within fourteen days with any recommendations, which the Assembly may accept or reject. If the Council does not return it in time, the Bill is deemed passed by both Houses as the Assembly passed it." },
  "199": { "s": "Defines a Money Bill; the Speaker's certificate is final.", "e": "A Bill is a Money Bill only if it deals solely with matters like imposing, abolishing or changing taxes, State borrowing or guarantees, custody of or payments into or out of the Consolidated or Contingency Fund, appropriation from the Consolidated Fund, charged expenditure, and related accounting matters. Bills that merely impose fines, licence fees or fees for services, or deal with local taxes, are not Money Bills for that reason alone. The Speaker's certificate that a Bill is a Money Bill is stated to be final, though in cases on the parallel Article 110 for Parliament the Supreme Court has indicated that such certification is not wholly beyond judicial review, and the question has been referred to a larger bench." },
  "200": { "s": "Governor's options on a passed Bill: assent, withhold, return, or reserve for the President.", "e": "When a Bill passed by the State Legislature reaches the Governor, he may give assent, withhold assent, or reserve it for the President's consideration. For non-Money Bills, he may instead return the Bill with a message asking the Legislature to reconsider it; if the Legislature passes it again, with or without changes, the Governor cannot withhold assent. He must reserve any Bill that would endanger the constitutional position of the High Court. The text says the Governor should act 'as soon as possible', but sets no fixed deadline, and long delays by Governors in several States led to litigation. In State of Punjab v. Principal Secretary to the Governor (2023), the Supreme Court held that a Governor who withholds assent must return the Bill to the Legislature and cannot simply sit on it. In State of Tamil Nadu v. Governor of Tamil Nadu (April 2025), a two-judge bench laid down time limits and treated certain long-pending Bills as deemed assented. In November 2025, answering a Presidential reference under Article 143, a five-judge Constitution Bench advised that courts cannot impose fixed timelines or declare deemed assent, though prolonged, unexplained inaction can be subject to limited judicial review (a direction to decide, without going into the merits)." },
  "201": { "s": "What the President does with a State Bill reserved by the Governor.", "e": "When the Governor reserves a Bill, the President (acting on Union ministerial advice) can assent or withhold assent. For non-Money Bills, the President can direct the Governor to return the Bill to the Legislature for reconsideration, which must happen within six months; if passed again, it goes back to the President, who is not bound to assent. No time limit is written into the article. The Supreme Court's April 2025 Tamil Nadu judgment set a three-month timeline for the President, but the November 2025 advisory opinion on the Presidential reference (see Article 200) held that courts cannot impose such timelines." },
  "202": { "s": "The Governor lays the State's annual budget before the Legislature.", "e": "Every financial year, the Governor must have the State's 'annual financial statement', the budget, laid before the Legislature, showing estimated receipts and spending. It must separate expenditure 'charged' on the Consolidated Fund (which is not voted on) from other expenditure, and revenue spending from other spending. Clause (3) lists charged items, such as the Governor's emoluments, debt charges, salaries of High Court judges and amounts needed to satisfy court judgments." },
  "203": { "s": "Charged expenditure is not voted on; other spending goes as demands for grants.", "e": "Charged expenditure may be discussed but is not put to the vote of the Assembly. All other spending is presented as 'demands for grants', which the Assembly may approve, reject or reduce, but not increase. A demand for grant can be made only on the Governor's recommendation, meaning only the government can propose spending. The Legislative Council has no vote on these demands." },
  "204": { "s": "An Appropriation Bill authorises withdrawal of money from the Consolidated Fund.", "e": "After the Assembly votes the grants, an Appropriation Bill is introduced to legally authorise withdrawal of money from the State's Consolidated Fund for those grants and for charged expenditure. No amendment can change the amount or purpose of a voted grant or charged spending. No money can be withdrawn from the Consolidated Fund except under such a law." },
  "205": { "s": "Supplementary, additional and excess grants when the budget proves insufficient.", "e": "If the money approved for a service turns out to be insufficient, or a new need arises during the year, or more was spent than granted, the Governor must place a supplementary statement or a demand for an excess before the Legislature. The same procedure as for the main budget (Articles 202–204) applies to these extra grants." },
  "206": { "s": "Vote on account, vote of credit and exceptional grants.", "e": "The Assembly can approve spending in advance for part of the year before the full budget is passed (vote on account), which is commonly used in election years. It can also make a lump-sum grant for an unexpected demand that cannot be detailed (vote of credit) and an exceptional grant outside the current year's services. The Legislature must then pass a law authorising withdrawal of these amounts from the Consolidated Fund." },
  "207": { "s": "Restrictions on introducing financial Bills: Governor's recommendation needed.", "e": "A Bill or amendment dealing with Money Bill matters (such as taxes or borrowing) needs the Governor's recommendation and cannot start in the Legislative Council, though no recommendation is needed for an amendment that reduces or abolishes a tax. Merely providing for fines or fees does not make a Bill financial. A Bill that would involve spending from the Consolidated Fund can be introduced in either House, but cannot be passed unless the Governor has recommended its consideration." },
  "208": { "s": "Each House makes its own rules of procedure.", "e": "Each House of a State Legislature can make rules for its procedure and conduct of business, subject to the Constitution. Until such rules were made, pre-Constitution provincial rules continued. In States with two Houses, the Governor, after consulting the Speaker and Chairman, makes rules for communication between the Houses." },
  "209": { "s": "State Legislature may regulate financial business procedure by law.", "e": "To ensure timely completion of budget and other financial business, the State Legislature can pass a law regulating procedure for financial matters. Where such a law conflicts with the House's own rules, the law prevails." },
  "210": { "s": "State Legislature business is conducted in the State language, Hindi or English.", "e": "Business in a State Legislature is transacted in the State's official language(s), Hindi or English. A member who cannot express himself adequately in these may be allowed by the presiding officer to speak in his mother tongue. The option of using English was to lapse after fifteen years unless the Legislature decided otherwise, though the period is twenty-five years for Himachal Pradesh, Manipur, Meghalaya and Tripura, and forty years for Arunachal Pradesh, Goa and Mizoram." },
  "211": { "s": "State Legislatures cannot discuss the conduct of judges in their duties.", "e": "No discussion can take place in a State Legislature about the conduct of any Supreme Court or High Court judge in performing judicial duties. This protects judicial independence. Unlike Parliament, a State Legislature has no role in removing judges, so there is no exception for a removal motion." },
  "212": { "s": "Courts cannot question legislative proceedings for mere procedural irregularity.", "e": "The validity of proceedings in a State Legislature cannot be challenged in court merely on the ground of an irregularity of procedure. Officers and members who regulate procedure or keep order are not subject to court jurisdiction for those acts. However, the Supreme Court has held that this protection does not cover illegality or unconstitutionality, so courts can still examine proceedings that are substantively unconstitutional (for example, Raja Ram Pal v. Hon'ble Speaker, 2007, under the parallel Article 122)." },
  "213": { "s": "Governor can issue Ordinances with the force of law when the Legislature is not in session.", "e": "When the Legislature (both Houses, where there are two) is not in session and the Governor is satisfied that immediate action is needed, he can issue an Ordinance with the same force as a State law. The Governor acts on ministerial advice, and needs the President's instructions where a Bill on the same subject would have needed Presidential sanction or assent. An Ordinance must be laid before the Legislature and lapses six weeks after it reassembles unless replaced by law, or earlier if disapproved; it cannot do anything a State law could not do. The 38th Amendment (1975) had made the Governor's satisfaction immune from court review, but the 44th Amendment (1978) removed that. In D.C. Wadhwa v. State of Bihar (1987) and Krishna Kumar Singh v. State of Bihar (2017), the Supreme Court held that repeatedly re-promulgating Ordinances without placing them before the Legislature is a 'fraud on the Constitution', and that Ordinances are subject to judicial review." },
  "214": { "s": "There shall be a High Court for each State.", "e": "Every State has a High Court, the highest court in the State. Some States share a common High Court under Article 231 (for example, Punjab, Haryana and Chandigarh). The original clauses on pre-existing High Courts were dropped by the Seventh Amendment (1956)." },
  "215": { "s": "High Courts are courts of record with power to punish contempt.", "e": "A 'court of record' is one whose judgments and proceedings are preserved as authoritative records and can be cited as precedent. Every High Court has this status and can punish for contempt of itself, a power the Supreme Court has held flows from the Constitution itself and is not created by the Contempt of Courts Act. High Courts also have power to punish contempt of subordinate courts." },
  "216": { "s": "A High Court consists of a Chief Justice and other judges fixed by the President.", "e": "Each High Court has a Chief Justice and as many other judges as the President considers necessary from time to time. Unlike the Supreme Court, where Parliament fixes the number by law, the strength of each High Court is fixed by executive decision." },
  "217": { "s": "Appointment, qualifications, tenure and removal of High Court judges.", "e": "High Court judges are appointed by the President and serve until age 62 (raised from 60 by the 15th Amendment, 1963). The 99th Amendment (2014) replaced the consultation process with a National Judicial Appointments Commission, and that wording still appears in the official text, but the Supreme Court struck it down in Supreme Court Advocates-on-Record Association v. Union of India (2015). As a result, appointments continue under the collegium system developed in the Second Judges Case (1993), in which the Chief Justice of India and senior judges have the decisive say. A candidate must be an Indian citizen who has held judicial office for ten years or been a High Court advocate for ten years. A judge may resign, or be removed only by the same impeachment process as a Supreme Court judge, and disputes about a judge's age are decided by the President after consulting the Chief Justice of India." },
  "218": { "s": "Supreme Court judges' removal rules also apply to High Court judges.", "e": "Clauses (4) and (5) of Article 124, which deal with removing Supreme Court judges for proved misbehaviour or incapacity through a special majority in both Houses of Parliament, apply equally to High Court judges. This gives High Court judges the same strong security of tenure." },
  "219": { "s": "High Court judges take an oath before the Governor.", "e": "Before taking office, every High Court judge takes an oath or affirmation in the form in the Third Schedule, before the Governor of the State or a person appointed by the Governor (in practice often the Chief Justice of the High Court)." },
  "220": { "s": "Retired permanent High Court judges can practise only in the Supreme Court and other High Courts.", "e": "After holding office as a permanent High Court judge, a person cannot plead or act before any court or authority in India except the Supreme Court and High Courts other than the one where he served. This prevents former judges from influencing courts they once supervised." },
  "221": { "s": "Salaries, allowances and pensions of High Court judges.", "e": "High Court judges' salaries are fixed by Parliament by law (since the 54th Amendment, 1986); before that, they were set out in the Second Schedule. Allowances, leave and pension are also set by Parliamentary law, and none of these can be changed to a judge's disadvantage after appointment, which protects judicial independence." },
  "222": { "s": "President may transfer a judge from one High Court to another.", "e": "The President can transfer a High Court judge to another High Court. The 99th Amendment's reference to the NJAC was struck down in 2015, so transfers continue to be made on the recommendation of the Supreme Court collegium, after consultation as laid down in the Judges Cases; the judge's consent is not required. A transferred judge receives a compensatory allowance in addition to salary." },
  "223": { "s": "President appoints an acting Chief Justice when the post is vacant.", "e": "When the Chief Justice's post is vacant, or the Chief Justice is absent or unable to act, the President appoints one of the other judges of that High Court to perform the duties. By convention, this is usually the senior-most puisne judge." },
  "224": { "s": "Appointment of additional judges and acting judges in High Courts.", "e": "If there is a temporary increase in work or a backlog, the President may appoint additional judges for up to two years. If a permanent judge is absent or is acting as Chief Justice, the President may appoint an acting judge until the permanent judge resumes. Additional and acting judges also retire at 62. The text refers to consultation with the NJAC, but since that was struck down in 2015, appointments are made through the collegium process." },
  "224A": { "s": "Retired judges may be asked to sit again as High Court judges.", "e": "With the President's consent, a retired High Court judge may be requested to sit and act as a judge of a High Court again, with all its jurisdiction and powers, though without being treated as a regular judge for other purposes. The retired judge must consent. The text refers to the NJAC, which was struck down in 2015, so in practice the request is initiated by the Chief Justice of the High Court. The Supreme Court in Lok Prahari v. Union of India (2021) encouraged use of this article to appoint ad hoc judges to tackle large backlogs and laid down guidelines for it." },
  "225": { "s": "Existing High Courts keep their jurisdiction and powers.", "e": "High Courts that existed before the Constitution continue with the same jurisdiction, law and powers, including making their own rules, subject to the Constitution and laws of the appropriate legislature. An old restriction that barred High Courts from hearing revenue matters in their original jurisdiction no longer applies. The 42nd Amendment had removed this proviso, but the 44th Amendment restored it." },
  "226": { "s": "High Courts can issue writs for fundamental rights and for any other purpose.", "e": "Every High Court can issue writs, orders and directions, including habeas corpus, mandamus, prohibition, quo warranto and certiorari, to any person or authority, including governments, within its territory. Unlike Article 32, which covers only fundamental rights, Article 226 covers enforcement of fundamental rights 'and for any other purpose', so it can be used to enforce legal rights and correct illegal administrative action, making it wider in scope. A High Court can act where the cause of action arises wholly or partly in its territory, even if the authority is based elsewhere. If an interim order is passed without hearing the other side, that party can apply to vacate it, and the order lapses if the application is not decided within two weeks. The 42nd Amendment (1976) curtailed this power, but the 43rd and 44th Amendments largely restored it, and the Supreme Court in L. Chandra Kumar v. Union of India (1997) held that High Courts' power of judicial review under Articles 226 and 227 is part of the basic structure." },
  "226A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly barred High Courts from deciding validity of Central laws in writ cases.", "e": "Inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976) during the Emergency, this article stopped High Courts from considering the constitutional validity of Central laws under Article 226. It was omitted by the 43rd Amendment (1977), restoring the High Courts' power." },
  "227": { "s": "High Courts supervise all courts and tribunals in their territory.", "e": "Every High Court has superintendence over all courts and tribunals in its territory, except military tribunals under the armed forces laws. It can call for returns, make rules and prescribe forms for practice and proceedings, and fix fees, subject to the Governor's approval and to existing laws. The 42nd Amendment (1976) removed tribunals from this supervision, but the 44th Amendment (1978) restored the original wording. Courts use this power to correct serious jurisdictional errors and grave injustice, but sparingly and not as an ordinary appeal; the Supreme Court in L. Chandra Kumar (1997) treated it as part of the basic structure." },
  "228": { "s": "High Court can take over cases raising substantial constitutional questions.", "e": "If a case pending in a lower court involves a substantial question of law about interpreting the Constitution that must be decided to dispose of the case, the High Court must withdraw it. It can then either decide the whole case itself, or decide the constitutional question and send the case back for the lower court to decide in line with its ruling. This keeps constitutional questions in the hands of the higher judiciary." },
  "228A": { "s": "Omitted — formerly special rules for deciding validity of State laws.", "e": "Inserted by the 42nd Amendment (1976), this article required special benches and majorities for High Courts to strike down State laws. It was omitted by the 43rd Amendment (1977)." },
  "229": { "s": "High Court's Chief Justice controls appointment of its staff; expenses are charged.", "e": "Appointments of officers and staff of a High Court are made by its Chief Justice or a judge or officer designated by him, though the Governor may require consultation with the State Public Service Commission in specified cases. Service conditions are set by rules made by the Chief Justice, with the Governor's approval needed for rules on salaries, allowances, leave and pensions. The High Court's administrative expenses, including staff salaries, are charged on the State's Consolidated Fund and are not voted on. This secures the High Court's administrative independence." },
  "230": { "s": "Parliament can extend or exclude a High Court's jurisdiction over Union territories.", "e": "Parliament can by law extend a High Court's jurisdiction to a Union territory or exclude it from one. When a State High Court covers a Union territory, the State Legislature cannot alter that jurisdiction, and the President, rather than the Governor, approves rules for courts in that territory. This was introduced by the Seventh Amendment (1956)." },
  "231": { "s": "Parliament can set up a common High Court for two or more States.", "e": "Parliament can establish a common High Court for two or more States, or for States and a Union territory, such as the Punjab and Haryana High Court and the Gauhati High Court. For such a court, rules for subordinate courts are approved by the Governor of the State where those courts are located, and references to 'the State' for oaths and staff refer to the State where the High Court has its principal seat." },
  "232": { "s": "Omitted — formerly an interpretation clause; replaced in 1956.", "e": "The Seventh Amendment (1956) replaced the old Articles 230, 231 and 232 with new Articles 230 and 231, so Article 232 no longer exists as a separate provision. The entry only records that substitution." },
  "233": { "s": "Governor appoints district judges in consultation with the High Court.", "e": "Appointment, posting and promotion of district judges are made by the Governor in consultation with the High Court. A person from outside government service can be appointed directly only if he has been an advocate or pleader for at least seven years and is recommended by the High Court. The Supreme Court has held that consultation with the High Court is mandatory and meaningful, and appointments made without it are invalid (Chandra Mohan v. State of U.P., 1966)." },
  "233A": { "s": "Validated certain district judge appointments and judgments made before 1966.", "e": "After the Supreme Court in Chandra Mohan v. State of U.P. (1966) held that some district judge appointments were invalid for not following Article 233, the 20th Amendment (1966) added this article. It validated those appointments, postings, promotions and transfers, and the judgments and orders passed by such judges, to avoid chaos in the justice system." },
  "234": { "s": "Recruitment of lower judicial officers under rules made after consulting PSC and High Court.", "e": "Appointments to the State judicial service below the level of district judge, such as civil judges and magistrates, are made by the Governor under rules framed after consulting the State Public Service Commission and the High Court. In practice, many States have the High Court itself conduct these recruitments." },
  "235": { "s": "High Court controls district courts and subordinate judges.", "e": "Control over district courts and all subordinate courts, including posting, promotion, leave and discipline of judicial officers below district judge, is vested in the High Court. This keeps the lower judiciary independent of the State Government. The Supreme Court has read 'control' broadly, including disciplinary control and premature retirement, while officers keep their statutory rights of appeal." },
  "236": { "s": "Defines \"district judge\" and \"judicial service\".", "e": "'District judge' includes city civil court judges, additional, joint and assistant district judges, chief judges of small cause courts, chief presidency magistrates and sessions judges of various grades. 'Judicial service' means a service made up only of people meant to fill district judge and lower civil judicial posts." },
  "237": { "s": "Governor may extend these judicial provisions to classes of magistrates.", "e": "The Governor can, by public notification, apply the provisions of this Chapter on subordinate courts to any class of magistrates, with modifications if needed. This was used to bring criminal magistrates under High Court control and separate the judiciary from the executive, as encouraged by Article 50." },
  "238": { "s": "Omitted — formerly applied Part VI to the Part B States.", "e": "Article 238 made the provisions of Part VI apply, with modifications, to the former Part B States (princely-state unions like Hyderabad and Mysore). It was omitted, together with the whole of Part VII, by the Seventh Amendment (1956), when the classification of States into Parts A, B, C and D was abolished." },
  "239": { "s": "Union territories are administered by the President through an Administrator.", "e": "Every Union territory is governed by the President, acting through an Administrator with whatever title the President chooses (such as Lieutenant Governor or Administrator), unless Parliament provides otherwise. The President may appoint the Governor of a neighbouring State as Administrator of a UT; the Governor then acts independently of his State's Council of Ministers, as with the Governor of Punjab administering Chandigarh." },
  "239A": { "s": "Parliament can create a legislature and/or council of ministers for Puducherry.", "e": "Parliament may by law create an elected or partly nominated legislature, a Council of Ministers, or both, for the Union territory of Puducherry. Originally added by the 14th Amendment (1962) for several territories, it now names only Puducherry. Such a law is not a constitutional amendment under Article 368. The Jammu and Kashmir Reorganisation Act, 2019 applies this article to the Union territory of Jammu and Kashmir as well." },
  "239AA": { "s": "Special status for Delhi: elected Assembly and ministry, with limits and Lieutenant Governor.", "e": "Added by the 69th Amendment (1991), this article renamed Delhi the National Capital Territory, with a Lieutenant Governor (LG), an elected Legislative Assembly and a Council of Ministers headed by a Chief Minister. The Assembly can make laws on State and Concurrent List matters except public order, police and land, but Parliament can also legislate for Delhi and its laws prevail. The Council (capped at 10% of the Assembly) aids and advises the LG on matters within the Assembly's power, but if they differ, the LG may refer the matter to the President, whose decision is binding. In State (NCT of Delhi) v. Union of India (2018), a Constitution Bench held that the LG is generally bound by ministerial advice and that the referral power is for exceptional matters, not every issue. In Government of NCT of Delhi v. Union of India (2023), another Constitution Bench held that the elected government controls 'services' (civil servants) except for public order, police and land. Parliament then passed the GNCTD (Amendment) Act, 2023, which places control over services with a National Capital Civil Service Authority and ultimately the LG; the Delhi Government's challenge to it, referred to a Constitution Bench in 2023, was withdrawn with the Supreme Court's permission in May 2025 after a change of government. The 106th Amendment (2023) adds a one-third reservation for women in the Delhi Assembly, to take effect after delimitation following the next census." },
  "239AB": { "s": "President can suspend Delhi's constitutional set-up if it fails.", "e": "If the President is satisfied, on the LG's report or otherwise, that Delhi cannot be administered in line with Article 239AA, or that this is necessary for its proper administration, he can suspend any provision of Article 239AA or laws made under it. This is Delhi's equivalent of President's Rule under Article 356." },
  "239B": { "s": "Puducherry's Administrator can issue Ordinances when its legislature is in recess.", "e": "When the Puducherry legislature is not in session, the Administrator can issue Ordinances if immediate action is needed, but only after getting the President's instructions. No Ordinance can be issued while the legislature is dissolved or suspended. An Ordinance must be laid before the legislature and lapses six weeks after it reassembles unless approved. By Article 239AA(8), this also applies to Delhi and its LG." },
  "240": { "s": "President can make regulations for certain Union territories.", "e": "The President can make regulations for the peace, progress and good government of the Andaman and Nicobar Islands, Lakshadweep, Dadra and Nagar Haveli and Daman and Diu (merged in 2020), and Puducherry. These regulations can amend or repeal Acts of Parliament applying to the territory and have the same force as an Act of Parliament. For Puducherry, this power is available only while its legislature is dissolved or suspended." },
  "241": { "s": "Parliament can create or designate a High Court for a Union territory.", "e": "Parliament can by law set up a High Court for a Union territory, or declare a court there to be a High Court. The provisions on State High Courts apply with modifications Parliament may make. The Delhi High Court is an example, and the High Court of Jammu and Kashmir and Ladakh serves two Union territories." },
  "242": { "s": "Omitted — formerly special provisions for Coorg.", "e": "This article contained special provisions for the Chief Commissioner's province of Coorg. It was omitted by the Seventh Amendment (1956), when Coorg was merged into the State of Mysore (now Karnataka)." },
  "243": { "s": "Definitions for Part IX on Panchayats.", "e": "Part IX was added by the 73rd Amendment (1992, in force from April 1993) to give constitutional status to rural local government. This article defines key terms: 'Gram Sabha' (all registered voters of a village Panchayat area), 'intermediate level' (as notified by the Governor), 'Panchayat' (a rural self-government institution under Article 243B), 'population' (as per the last published census), and 'village'." },
  "243A": { "s": "The Gram Sabha exercises powers given by State law.", "e": "The Gram Sabha, made up of all adult voters of a village, is the base of the Panchayat system. Its powers and functions are whatever the State Legislature gives it by law, typically approving village plans, selecting beneficiaries of schemes and conducting social audits." },
  "243B": { "s": "Three-tier Panchayats at village, intermediate and district levels.", "e": "Every State must set up Panchayats at the village, intermediate (block) and district levels. States with a population of twenty lakh or less may skip the intermediate level." },
  "243C": { "s": "Composition of Panchayats: direct elections and chairpersons.", "e": "The State Legislature decides the make-up of Panchayats, keeping the population-to-seat ratio roughly uniform across the State. All seats are filled by direct election from territorial constituencies. State law can provide for chairpersons of lower Panchayats, MPs and MLAs to be represented at intermediate and district levels, and they can vote in meetings. Chairpersons at village level are chosen as State law provides, and at intermediate and district levels are elected by and from the elected members." },
  "243D": { "s": "Reservation in Panchayats for SCs, STs and women, including chairperson posts.", "e": "Seats in every Panchayat are reserved for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in proportion to their population in the area, and can be rotated among constituencies. At least one-third of all directly elected seats, including within the SC and ST quotas, are reserved for women, also by rotation. Chairperson posts at every level are reserved for SCs, STs and women as State law provides, with at least one-third for women. SC/ST reservations last as long as those in Article 334, while women's reservation has no such end date. States may also reserve seats and chairperson posts for backward classes; the Supreme Court upheld this in K. Krishna Murthy v. Union of India (2010), and in Vikas Kishanrao Gawali v. State of Maharashtra (2021) required a 'triple test' (a dedicated commission, local-body-wise data, and a 50% overall ceiling) before such OBC reservation. Many States have chosen to raise women's reservation in Panchayats to 50%." },
  "243E": { "s": "Panchayats have a five-year term; elections must be held on time.", "e": "Every Panchayat lasts five years from its first meeting unless dissolved earlier under law. Changing a law cannot dissolve a functioning Panchayat before its term ends. Elections must be completed before the term expires, or within six months of an early dissolution. A Panchayat elected after early dissolution serves only the remainder of the original term." },
  "243F": { "s": "Disqualifications for Panchayat members.", "e": "A person is disqualified from Panchayat membership if disqualified under laws for State Legislature elections, except that age 21 rather than 25 is sufficient, or under a State law. States can add conditions: the Supreme Court upheld a two-child norm in Javed v. State of Haryana (2003) and minimum educational qualifications in Rajbala v. State of Haryana (2015). Disputes about disqualification are decided by an authority named in State law." },
  "243G": { "s": "States may give Panchayats powers to function as self-government, including Eleventh Schedule subjects.", "e": "This is the heart of Panchayati Raj, but it is enabling rather than mandatory. The State Legislature 'may' give Panchayats the powers and authority needed to function as institutions of self-government. Such laws can devolve responsibility for preparing plans for economic development and social justice and implementing schemes, including on the 29 subjects in the Eleventh Schedule, such as agriculture, drinking water, rural roads, primary education, health and poverty alleviation. Because the degree of devolution is left to each State, the actual powers, funds and staff of Panchayats vary widely across India. This article must be read with Articles 243H and 243I on finance, without which devolution remains weak." },
  "243H": { "s": "States may let Panchayats levy taxes and receive grants.", "e": "The State Legislature can authorise Panchayats to levy and collect taxes, duties, tolls and fees, assign them State-collected taxes, give grants-in-aid from the State's Consolidated Fund, and create Panchayat funds. This gives Panchayats a financial base, though the extent depends on State law." },
  "243I": { "s": "State Finance Commission every five years to review Panchayat finances.", "e": "The Governor must set up a State Finance Commission every five years to review Panchayat finances. It recommends how State taxes should be shared with Panchayats, which taxes Panchayats can levy or receive, and what grants they should get. The Governor places its report and an action-taken note before the Legislature." },
  "243J": { "s": "State law governs Panchayat accounts and audit.", "e": "The State Legislature can make laws on how Panchayats keep accounts and how those accounts are audited, to ensure financial accountability." },
  "243K": { "s": "A State Election Commission conducts Panchayat elections.", "e": "All Panchayat elections, including preparing electoral rolls, are conducted under the superintendence of a State Election Commission headed by a State Election Commissioner appointed by the Governor. The Commissioner can be removed only like a High Court judge, and his service conditions cannot be changed to his disadvantage. The Supreme Court has stressed that State Election Commissions must be independent and that elections must be held on time." },
  "243L": { "s": "Part IX applies to Union territories, with modifications by the President.", "e": "The Panchayat provisions apply to Union territories, with the Administrator taking the place of the Governor, and the UT's Legislative Assembly, if any, taking the place of the State Legislature. The President can apply them with exceptions and modifications." },
  "243M": { "s": "Part IX does not apply to certain tribal and hill areas.", "e": "The Panchayat provisions do not apply to Scheduled Areas and tribal areas under Article 244, to Nagaland, Meghalaya and Mizoram, or to hill areas of Manipur with District Councils, and district-level provisions do not apply to the Darjeeling hill areas. The 83rd Amendment (2000) excluded Arunachal Pradesh from SC reservation in Panchayats since it has almost no SC population. The excluded States can adopt Part IX by a special resolution, and Parliament can extend it to Scheduled Areas, as it did through the Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act, 1996 (PESA)." },
  "243N": { "s": "Old Panchayat laws continued for one year after the 73rd Amendment.", "e": "State laws on Panchayats inconsistent with Part IX could continue for up to one year from the 73rd Amendment (April 1993), unless amended earlier. Existing Panchayats could complete their terms unless dissolved by a resolution of the State Legislature." },
  "243O": { "s": "Courts cannot interfere in Panchayat delimitation; election disputes go by petition.", "e": "Laws on delimiting Panchayat constituencies or allotting seats cannot be challenged in court. A Panchayat election can only be challenged by an election petition before the authority set by State law. Courts have read this to bar interference once the election process has started, though they may intervene to ensure elections are held." },
  "243P": { "s": "Definitions for Part IXA on Municipalities.", "e": "Part IXA was added by the 74th Amendment (1992, in force from June 1993) to give constitutional status to urban local bodies. This article defines terms such as 'Metropolitan area' (a population of ten lakh or more, notified by the Governor), 'Municipal area', 'Municipality' and 'population'." },
  "243Q": { "s": "Three types of Municipalities: Nagar Panchayat, Municipal Council, Municipal Corporation.", "e": "Every State must constitute a Nagar Panchayat for areas transitioning from rural to urban, a Municipal Council for smaller urban areas and a Municipal Corporation for larger urban areas. The Governor decides classification based on population, revenue, employment and similar factors. An area served by an industrial establishment may be notified as an industrial township, without a Municipality." },
  "243R": { "s": "Composition of Municipalities: elected ward members plus some others.", "e": "All seats are filled by direct election from wards. State law can also provide for nominated persons with municipal expertise (who cannot vote), MPs, MLAs and MLCs representing the area, and Wards Committee chairpersons. The State law decides how the Chairperson is elected." },
  "243S": { "s": "Wards Committees in Municipalities with population of three lakh or more.", "e": "Municipalities with a population of three lakh or more must have Wards Committees, each covering one or more wards. The ward's elected member chairs a single-ward committee, and the State law decides membership and chairpersons for multi-ward committees. States can also create other committees." },
  "243T": { "s": "Reservation in Municipalities for SCs, STs, women and optionally backward classes.", "e": "Seats in every Municipality are reserved for SCs and STs in proportion to population, and at least one-third of all directly elected seats, including within SC/ST quotas, for women, by rotation. Chairperson posts may be reserved as State law provides. SC/ST reservation ends with Article 334's period, and States can also reserve for backward classes, subject to the conditions set in K. Krishna Murthy (2010) and the triple test in Vikas Kishanrao Gawali (2021)." },
  "243U": { "s": "Municipalities have a five-year term; elections must be on time.", "e": "A Municipality lasts five years and must be heard before any early dissolution. A change in law cannot cut short an existing Municipality's term. Elections must be held before the term ends or within six months of dissolution. In Kishansing Tomar v. Municipal Corporation of Ahmedabad (2006), the Supreme Court held that the State Election Commission must ensure elections are held on time." },
  "243V": { "s": "Disqualifications for Municipality members.", "e": "A person is disqualified from Municipality membership if disqualified for State Legislature elections, though age 21 is sufficient, or under a State law. Disputes are decided by an authority under State law." },
  "243W": { "s": "States may give Municipalities powers, including Twelfth Schedule functions.", "e": "The State Legislature may give Municipalities and committees the powers needed to function as self-governing institutions. This includes preparing plans for economic development and social justice and carrying out functions such as the 18 subjects in the Twelfth Schedule (urban planning, water supply, public health, sanitation, slum improvement and others). As with Panchayats, the actual extent of devolution depends on each State." },
  "243X": { "s": "States may let Municipalities levy taxes and receive grants.", "e": "The State Legislature can authorise Municipalities to levy and collect taxes, duties, tolls and fees, assign them State taxes, give grants-in-aid, and create municipal funds. Municipal finances, such as property tax, therefore depend on State law." },
  "243Y": { "s": "State Finance Commission also reviews Municipal finances.", "e": "The State Finance Commission set up under Article 243I also reviews municipal finances and recommends how State taxes should be shared with Municipalities, which taxes they may levy and grants they should receive. Its report and action taken are placed before the Legislature." },
  "243Z": { "s": "State law governs municipal accounts and audit.", "e": "The State Legislature can make laws on how Municipalities keep accounts and how those accounts are audited." },
  "243ZA": { "s": "State Election Commission conducts municipal elections.", "e": "The State Election Commission under Article 243K also conducts elections to Municipalities, including electoral rolls. The State Legislature can make laws on other election-related matters." },
  "243ZB": { "s": "Part IXA applies to Union territories.", "e": "The Municipality provisions apply to Union territories, with the Administrator replacing the Governor and the UT Assembly, if any, replacing the State Legislature. The President may apply them with exceptions and modifications." },
  "243ZC": { "s": "Part IXA does not apply to Scheduled and tribal areas.", "e": "The Municipality provisions do not apply to Scheduled Areas and tribal areas under Article 244, and do not affect the Darjeeling Gorkha Hill Council. Parliament may extend them to these areas by law with modifications, which is not treated as a constitutional amendment." },
  "243ZD": { "s": "District Planning Committee consolidates Panchayat and municipal plans.", "e": "Every State must constitute a District Planning Committee to combine the plans of Panchayats and Municipalities into a draft development plan for the whole district. At least four-fifths of its members must be elected by and from elected members of the district Panchayat and Municipalities, in proportion to the rural and urban populations. The draft plan is sent to the State Government." },
  "243ZE": { "s": "Metropolitan Planning Committee for each Metropolitan area.", "e": "Every Metropolitan area must have a Metropolitan Planning Committee to prepare a development plan for the whole area. At least two-thirds of its members are elected by and from elected municipal members and Panchayat chairpersons in the area. It must consider common needs such as water, infrastructure and environmental conservation, and its draft plan goes to the State Government." },
  "243ZF": { "s": "Old municipal laws continued for one year after the 74th Amendment.", "e": "State municipal laws inconsistent with Part IXA could continue for up to one year from the 74th Amendment (June 1993), unless amended earlier. Existing Municipalities could complete their terms unless dissolved by a State Legislature resolution." },
  "243ZG": { "s": "Courts cannot interfere in municipal delimitation; election disputes go by petition.", "e": "Laws on delimiting municipal constituencies or allotting seats cannot be challenged in court. A municipal election can be challenged only by an election petition before the authority named in State law." },
  "243ZH": { "s": "Definitions for Part IXB on co-operative societies.", "e": "Part IXB was added by the 97th Amendment (2011, in force from February 2012), which also made forming co-operative societies a fundamental right under Article 19(1)(c) and added Article 43B. This article defines terms like 'board', 'co-operative society', 'multi-State co-operative society', 'office bearer' and 'Registrar'. In Union of India v. Rajendra N. Shah (2021), the Supreme Court struck down Part IXB to the extent it relates to co-operative societies within a single State, because co-operatives are a State subject and the amendment was not ratified by half the States; the Part now operates only for multi-State co-operative societies." },
  "243ZI": { "s": "States may make laws on co-operatives based on democratic principles.", "e": "The State Legislature may make laws on forming, regulating and winding up co-operative societies based on voluntary formation, democratic member control, member economic participation and autonomy. After Rajendra N. Shah (2021), this binds only in relation to multi-State co-operatives (through Article 243ZR); State co-operatives are governed by State laws." },
  "243ZJ": { "s": "Co-operative boards: at most 21 directors, reservations, five-year term.", "e": "A co-operative board can have at most 21 directors, with one seat for SCs or STs and two for women where applicable. Elected members and office bearers serve five years. Up to two experts can be co-opted but cannot vote in elections or become office bearers. Since 2021, this applies only to multi-State co-operative societies." },
  "243ZK": { "s": "Co-operative board elections must be held before the term ends.", "e": "Board elections must be held before the outgoing board's term ends so the new board takes over immediately. Elections are conducted by an authority set up by law. After Rajendra N. Shah (2021), this binds only for multi-State co-operative societies." },
  "243ZL": { "s": "Limits on superseding or suspending co-operative boards.", "e": "A co-operative board cannot be superseded or suspended for more than six months, or one year for co-operative banks other than multi-State ones, and only for defined reasons such as persistent default, negligence or stalemate. A board cannot be superseded at all if the society has no government shareholding, loan or guarantee. The administrator must hold elections within the period. Since 2021, this applies only to multi-State co-operatives." },
  "243ZM": { "s": "Annual audit of co-operative accounts by qualified auditors.", "e": "Co-operative accounts must be audited at least once a year by qualified auditors appointed by the general body from an approved panel, within six months of the financial year's close. Apex co-operatives' audit reports must be laid before the Legislature. Since 2021, this binds only for multi-State co-operatives." },
  "243ZN": { "s": "Annual general meeting within six months of the financial year's end.", "e": "The law may require every co-operative's annual general body meeting to be held within six months of the financial year's end. After Rajendra N. Shah (2021), this applies only to multi-State co-operative societies." },
  "243ZO": { "s": "Members' right to information and participation in co-operatives.", "e": "The law may give every member access to the society's books and accounts relating to their dealings, require minimum participation in meetings and services, and provide co-operative education and training. Since 2021, this applies only to multi-State co-operatives." },
  "243ZP": { "s": "Co-operatives must file annual returns within six months.", "e": "Every co-operative society must file annual returns with the designated authority within six months of the financial year's end, including its annual report, audited accounts, surplus disposal plan, bye-law amendments and details of its general meeting and elections. Since 2021, this binds only multi-State co-operatives." },
  "243ZQ": { "s": "Offences and penalties relating to co-operative societies.", "e": "The law must make certain acts offences, such as filing false returns, disobeying lawful summons, wrongful employer deductions, failure to hand over records, and corrupt practices during elections. After Rajendra N. Shah (2021), this applies only to multi-State co-operative societies." },
  "243ZR": { "s": "Part IXB applies to multi-State co-operatives through Parliament and the Centre.", "e": "For multi-State co-operative societies, all references to the State Legislature, State Act and State Government are read as Parliament, Central Act and Central Government. This is the part of Part IXB that survives after Rajendra N. Shah (2021); the Multi-State Co-operative Societies Act, 2002 (amended in 2023) is the main Central law." },
  "243ZS": { "s": "Part IXB applies to Union territories.", "e": "The co-operative provisions apply to Union territories, with the Administrator or the UT's Assembly taking the role of the State Legislature. The President may, by notification, exclude any UT or part of one. After the Supreme Court's 2021 ruling in Rajendra N. Shah, Part IXB applies in Union territories only to multi-State co-operative societies, not to co-operatives confined to a single Union territory." },
  "243ZT": { "s": "Old co-operative laws continued for one year after the 97th Amendment.", "e": "State co-operative laws inconsistent with Part IXB could continue for up to one year from the 97th Amendment's commencement (February 2012) unless amended earlier. After the 2021 Rajendra N. Shah ruling, co-operatives within a single State are governed by State laws without the constraints of Part IXB." }
});
